7. Pointers - Variables that store addresses

    A pointer stores the adress of an object or function, pedending on its type.

            int     age;

            age = 20;

            int     *ptr;

            ptr = &age;

    age     stores the integer 20,
    &age    gives the adress of age,
    ptr     stores the address,
    *ptr    access the value at that adress

		Examle:

            printf("%d\n", *ptr); // 20

            *ptr = 25;
            printf("%d\n", age);  // 25

    Pointers are essential for dynamic memory, arrays, strings, and working with functions that need to modify data.
